From: well0nez <8986239+well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: arm64: dts: h713: hy320: PB5 to the board manager, so a standby switches lamp and fan off

The tree kept PB5 as the dtsi's gpio-hog and took fan-power-gpio away
from the board manager, which then says "fan power rail not ours, left as
it is" at suspend: the fan and the lamp would keep running in standby, as
on the HY300PRO-G-EU before 0249. The board's stock tree names PB5 as
tvtop panel_bl_en, a GPIO, as the HY310's does, so the board manager owns
it as there: off late in suspend, on again at resume. Unmeasured on this
board.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
diff -u a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713-hy320.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713-hy320.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713-hy320.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713-hy320.dts
@@ -21,8 +21,8 @@
  *  - The fan: the stock "fan" node claims PH17 twice, as PWM0's pin and as
  *    fan1-fg-gpio, and U-Boot's fan_ctrl names PH17 again. On this unit
  *    PH17 is the tacho (an EINT, 3900 RPM; 01.11.2026). So the dtsi's fan0
- *    stays: tacho PH17, no PWM, the fan on PB5. PB5 stays the dtsi's hog,
- *    as on the HY300 Pro.
+ *    stays: tacho PH17, no PWM, the fan on PB5. PB5 is the board
+ *    manager's, as on the HY310 (below).
  *  - No NTC evaluation, as under stock: the stock fan driver reads GPADC
  *    channel 1 only while LRADC channel 0 reads 5 or less, and here it
  *    reads 63; channel 1 itself scatters like the HY310's open input.
@@ -81,12 +81,14 @@
 };
 
 /*
- * PB5 enables the LED boost and the fan rail (stock tvtop panel_bl_en, a
- * GPIO): the dtsi's hog holds it high, not the board manager - as on the
- * HY300 Pro, so nothing in Linux ever drives it low.
+ * PB5 enables the lamp and the fan rail together (stock tvtop
+ * panel_bl_en, a GPIO). The board manager owns it as on the HY310 and the
+ * HY200 QZ713DF A1 (0249), so a standby switches lamp and fan off and back
+ * on; with the hog they kept running in standby. Until the board manager
+ * probes, PB5 keeps the high level U-Boot set.
  */
 &fan_power_hog {
-	status = "okay";
+	status = "disabled";
 };
 
 /*
@@ -122,7 +124,7 @@
 	io-channels = <&gpadc 1>;
 
 	fan0 {
-		/delete-property/ fan-power-gpio;
+		fan-power-gpio = <&pio 1 5 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;	/* PB5 */
 	};
 };
 
